# Facebook ads low-budget guide (reference only)

Source: `Mejor_Vida_FB_Ads_Low_Budget_Guide.pdf` (Sergio Cardenas video, adapted for Mejor Vida, September 25, 2026). Use this when Julie asks how a test, metric, or budget change should be handled. Do not rebuild the live system from it.

## The built system stays

Ad Builder stages, `hooks.json`, and `ad-photos.json` stay as already built. Stage 1 is many images with one hook and one wording. Stage 2 is one image with many hooks and many wordings. Stage 3 is one image, one hook, and many wordings. Do not shrink those stages to the guide’s 3–4 live ads unless she asks.

## Reference rules

- Creative first, then WhatsApp destination, then audience, then budget. Change one of those at a time.
- Current practice (October 2026): about **$50/day** to get a read faster. Each Stage 1/2/3 test ad **auto-pauses in Meta at 750 lifetime impressions** (CRM warns near 600). Julie may turn one off earlier if it is clearly good or bad. Cost per sale is too thin at 750 impressions to name a winner.
- The original low-budget guide used about $20/day and 3–4 live ads. Do not drop back to $20 unless she asks.
- Do not name a winner from CTR (all) or CPM. Order is: compliance, comparable delivery, cost per messaging conversation and link CTR, qualified chats and appointments, then applications and placed policies.
- A WhatsApp click is not a lead. A started chat is not yet qualified. ManyChat completion teaches Meta only if that event is sent back.
- Keep a control running. Test challengers separately. If the daily budget is raised again, step it up gradually and watch.
- One hostile comment is not a verdict. Repeated confusion or a misleading claim is a reason to revise.
